mode: "100644"
text: "# Card types, checklists & worked examples\n\n## Text-graphic types (pick the fit)\n| Type | What it frames | Source it pulls from |\n|---|---|---|\n| Original-line card | the brand's own sticky line | hook-writer, contrarian, text-post-and-microblog |\n| Attributed quote card | a real third-party quote (in context) | verified source (human confirms wording) |\n| Stat card | one real, sourced number | data-and-original-research (a lone stat routes here) |\n| Testimonial card | a real, consented customer quote | social-proof-and-testimonials (FTC rules) |\n| Pull-quote / carousel set | a series of lines in one system | design-and-templates (consistent set) |\n\n## The quotability test (before you design)\nDoes the line stand alone (no surrounding context needed)? · Would someone save/re-share it? · Is it short\nenough to breathe? · Is it a claim/reframe/truth, not a mundane statement? · Is it TRUE and, if attributed,\nverifiable? If it fails, sharpen the line — don't dress up a weak one.\n\n## The legibility checklist (before you publish)\nMain quote ≥ ~24pt ✓ · high contrast (overlay/panel over any photo) ✓ · two fonts max ✓ · key phrase leads ✓ ·\nline breaks on meaning, not mid-word ✓ · safe margins ✓ · attribution present + correct ✓ · reads clearly at\nthumb-size on a real phone ✓.\n\n## Worked example A — \"Blunt indie founder\" (original-line card)\nLine: \"You don't need more followers. You need more trust.\" Spec: black card, one bold sans, \"more trust\" in the\naccent color and largest weight, small \"— [brand handle]\" bottom-left, generous margins. One idea, outsized,\non-brand, thumb-legible. Attributed to the brand (safe + honest).\n\n## Worked example B — \"Warm bookkeeping studio\" (stat card, sourced)\nLine: big \"96%\" + \"of our monthly-plan clients filed on time last year\" + small \"source: our 2025 client base,\nn=180.\" Warm cream card, serif for the number, one accent. Real number, sourced on the card, no rounding games.\n(If it had been unsourced, we'd get the source before rendering.)\n\n## The line-break craft (the pro tell)\nWhere you break the lines changes how a quote reads. Break on the unit of meaning so each line is a coherent\nphrase, land the strongest word at the end of a line (it gets the most weight), and never hyphenate or split\nmid-word to fit. A well-broken three-line quote reads as designed; a badly-broken one reads as a screenshot.\n\n## Never\nFrame a line that isn't quotable · fabricate a quote · attribute an invented/fictional line to a real person ·\nput words in a competitor's mouth · trim a quote to flip its meaning · reproduce copyrighted lyrics/poems · put a\ntiny quote over a busy photo · fabricate a stat/source · claim WoopSocial generates the image (a design tool does).\n"
